<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The curation tool's bundle manifest. `tools/dist/cmd.sh` substitutes @VERSION@ and copies this in;
  everything else is fixed.

  A second plist rather than a parameterised one. It used to say the reason was that this bundle
  declares a document type and the machine has none -- which stopped being true when the machine
  claimed `.kmpkg`. The reason that survives is smaller and still sufficient: two bundles, two
  identifiers, two icons, two names and two document types, with almost nothing shared but the
  version string that is substituted into both.

  The two declarations are worth reading side by side, because they differ where the products do.
  This one is an `Editor` of its type; the machine is a `Viewer` of its own, since installing a
  package is not writing one. And the delivery mechanism differs underneath: `tao`'s delegate
  receives the Apple Event here, while the machine gets it as an SDL drop event -- see the note in
  `Info.plist`.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>KM Package Builder</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>KM Package Builder</string>
    <!-- Distinct from `com.karaokemachine.app`. Two bundles cannot share an identifier: LaunchServices
         keys its whole database on it, and a collision means one of them silently replaces the
         other. -->
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>com.karaokemachine.packagebuilder</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>km-package-builder</string>
    <!-- Without extension. macOS looks for `<this>.icns` in Contents/Resources.

         The tool's own mark: the theme's accent blue, the same drawing the machine wears in amber.
         Written by crates/playback/km-display/examples/icon.rs and staged by tools/dist/cmd.sh, which is
         also what makes this bundle at all. This string is the file's name without its extension,
         so renaming icon/km-package-builder.icns means changing it here -- and in the two icon keys
         further down, which name the same file for the document rather than for the application. -->
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>km-package-builder</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>@VERSION@</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>@VERSION@</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>11.0</string>
    <!-- Utilities rather than Music: this plays nothing. It reads a folder of somebody's songs
         and writes a package -- a tool about files, which is what that shelf is for. -->
    <key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
    <string>public.app-category.utilities</string>
    <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      The document type, which is the whole reason this bundle exists rather than a bare executable.
      **Both halves are needed and they do different jobs.**

      `CFBundleDocumentTypes` says "this application opens files of this type". On its own that is a
      claim about a type nobody has defined, so it does nothing.

      `UTExportedTypeDeclarations` is the definition: it says the type exists, that `.kmbuild` is how
      you recognize it, and that it is ordinary data. Exported rather than imported because this
      product invents the type -- an imported declaration is for describing somebody else's format.

      What follows from having them is the reason for the whole macOS branch of this milestone: a
      double-clicked document is delivered to a *running* application as a `kAEOpenDocuments` Apple
      Event, never in `argv`. `tao`'s application delegate is what receives it. See src/desktop.rs.
    -->
    <key>CFBundleDocumentTypes</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>CFBundleTypeName</key>
            <string>Karaoke corpus</string>
            <key>LSItemContentTypes</key>
            <array>
                <string>com.karaokemachine.kmbuild</string>
            </array>
            <!-- Editor rather than Viewer: opening a corpus is how you change it. -->
            <key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
            <string>Editor</string>
            <!-- Owner, because this product defines the type and nothing else can read it. -->
            <key>LSHandlerRank</key>
            <string>Owner</string>
            <key>CFBundleTypeIconFile</key>
            <string>km-package-builder</string>
        </dict>
    </array>

    <key>UTExportedTypeDeclarations</key>
    <array>
        <dict>
            <key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
            <string>com.karaokemachine.kmbuild</string>
            <key>UTTypeDescription</key>
            <string>Karaoke corpus</string>
            <!-- `public.data` rather than `public.database`: the latter is not a system type, and a
                 conformance to something undefined is silently ignored. What matters to the system is
                 that this is an opaque file. -->
            <key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
            <array>
                <string>public.data</string>
            </array>
            <key>UTTypeIconFile</key>
            <string>km-package-builder</string>
            <key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
            <dict>
                <key>public.filename-extension</key>
                <array>
                    <string>kmbuild</string>
                </array>
            </dict>
        </dict>
    </array>

    <!--
      **Why this tool asks for file access, in its own words.**

      A bundle has a TCC identity of its own, and that is the surprise a folder does not prepare you
      for: the same executable run from a terminal inherits the terminal's grants and is never asked
      about anything, while the `.app` is asked the first time it looks at a Desktop, a Documents
      folder, a Downloads folder or an external drive. Found by opening this bundle for the first
      time -- it stops on the prompt before it serves a page, so the window is white until somebody
      answers.

      These four keys do not remove the prompt and are not meant to. What they do is make it say why:
      with no string macOS shows a bare "would like to access files in your Desktop folder", which is
      a question about a program you have just unzipped with nothing to judge it by. Four keys and not
      one, because they are asked separately and a karaoke corpus genuinely lives in any of them --
      Downloads for something just fetched, and an external drive for a collection too big for the
      internal one.

      Still no microphone, camera or location: this tool reads karaoke files and writes packages.
      Declaring a permission it never uses would make macOS prompt for one.
    -->
    <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>KM Package Builder reads the karaoke files in the folder you choose, so that you can browse and curate them into packages.</string>
    <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>KM Package Builder reads the karaoke files in the folder you choose, so that you can browse and curate them into packages.</string>
    <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>KM Package Builder reads the karaoke files in the folder you choose, so that you can browse and curate them into packages.</string>
    <key>NSRemovableVolumeUsageDescription</key>
    <string>KM Package Builder reads the karaoke files in the folder you choose, which is often a collection kept on an external drive.</string>

    <!--
      **Added when the offline remote got a bundle of its own**, because writing that one's manifest is
      what turned this up here. Since M29 this tool has a Discover button that browses
      `_karaokemachine._tcp.local.` for machines, and it installs packages onto whichever one is set --
      so it sends to a local address, which macOS 14 and later ask about. The prompt was already being
      raised by a bundle that said nothing about why.

      Declining it costs the Discover button and the install, and nothing else: scanning, browsing,
      rating and building a package are all local work.
    -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>KM Package Builder looks for your karaoke machine on this network, so that it can test-play a song and install the packages you build.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
